    [Ed. comment - I note with interest that most of the people responding
    negatively to Lorrie's (sorry to use the familiar form; I can't call up
    the orignal post as I reply) comments are men ... ;) ]

    As another woman 'in the field', I'd like to respond to some of the
    comments that I'm seeing here:

	I have been encouraged by the growing number of "women-only" or
    "women-centered" lists, newsgroups, etc. for women in fields such as
    computer science or electrical engineering.  It is a sad fact that for
    a long time, women were not encouraged to enter these fields.  Computer
    technology is not an inherently 'gendered' technology; that is, there
    is no physically limiting reason that dictates that this kind of work
    is more easily done by men than women or vice-versa.  Yet, time and
    time again, people have been surprised to learn that I know anything
    about computers, let alone am pursuing a computer science major.  

    	I don't think that these "women-only" groups are necessarily
    operating on the idea that "men are EVIL and we shall purge them from
    our ranks, BUHAHAHA".  To a large extent, this is more of a special
    interest group that discusses experiences, etc. that have a
    particularly strong resonance for women.  The restriction of such
    groups is an attempt to filter out those who would belittle these
    experiences.  My experiences on the net tell me that there are A LOT
    of people who discriminate, ranging from making the occasional
    uninformed comment to flagrant shows of bigotry.  Yes, I know that
    these people have a right to express themselves, even if it is to tell
    me that I'm just being a hysterical, emotional female.  ;)  Yes, I know 
    that it is impossible to completely filter out these people from
    "women-only" groups.  But to claim that these groups have NO RIGHT to
    police themselves strikes me as hypocritical in the face of other forms
    of self-control on the Net.  If someone decided to post Amiga software
    reviews to  rec.org.sca or felt an urge to send out nursery rhymes to
    the Virtual Reality mailing list (which is moderated and on a LISTSERV,
    I believe), I doubt that many people would argue that the moderator or
    admin would act soley as a censor when he/she removed that material or
    set up system where that material was not relayed to the entire group.  

	I don't believe that restricting such groups to "women-only" is the
    aboslutely the best way to ensure a environment condusive to
    communication rather than "bashing" or "flaming".  Men are not
    necessarily jerks; women are not neccessarily angels of compassion.  I've 
    worked with men who couldn't have cared less about the biological facts of 
    my being and were both interested in and supportive of issues that often 
    impact more heavily on women than on men, such as maternity leave policies 
    of various companies, the increased hiring of _capable_ women (as
    opposed to hiring  simply to fill 'quota' ...) for professional
    positions, or how incidents of sexual harrasment are handled at work. 
    I tend to operate on the belief that until you prove otherwise, I will
    consider you a semi-rational, intelligent human being with a sincere
    interest in communicating with me, rather than talking AT me or
    converting me, etc.  But I have seen -- and had -- experiences that help me
    understand why some people may operate differently.                 



